<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kreva</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Krewo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kreva is a village in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a> Region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, known for the ruins of its fourteenth-century castle, one of the oldest stone castles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kestutis">Kęstutis</a> died there in captivity in 1382, held by his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>. In 1385 the castle gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-krewo">Union of Krewo</a>, which prepared Jogaila's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadwiga-of-poland">Queen Jadwiga</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kreva.jpg" alt="Kreva" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DBatura · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%90%D0%B3%D0%BB%D1%8F%D0%B4_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9A%D1%80%D1%8D%D0%B2%D0%B0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
